    "slug": "maze-generation-bias",
    "title": "There are 192 mazes on a 3×3 grid. The recursive backtracker can build 14.",
    "summary": "Kirchhoff's 1847 determinant counts them, and a 3×3 grid of cells holds exactly 192 perfect mazes. Two of the three builders below can produce every one of them. The third reaches fourteen, and the missing 178 are not rare — they are unreachable.",
    "date": "2026-08-18",
    "embed": "maze-weaver",
    "embedCaption": "Press Solve and read the last figure in the status line — \"path N cells\". Now press Randomized Prim: it rebuilds the same seed, so only the rule has changed. Solve again. The corner-to-corner route gets shorter every time, usually by a factor of three or four, and one long snaking corridor is replaced by a bush of short dead ends.",
    "published": true,
    "seoTitle": "Maze Generation Algorithms: Which Mazes They Cannot Build",
    "metaDescription": "Recursive backtracker, Prim and Kruskal all make a perfect maze — but not the same set. On a 3×3 grid the backtracker reaches 14 of the 192 that exist.",
    "keywords": "maze generation algorithm, recursive backtracker maze, randomized prim maze, randomized kruskal maze, wilson's algorithm maze, aldous broder algorithm, uniform spanning tree, perfect maze, number of spanning trees grid graph, kirchhoff matrix tree theorem, oeis a007341, dfs palm tree tarjan 1972, maze algorithm bias, mazes for programmers",
    "content": "In 1847 Gustav Kirchhoff was twenty-three and had just finished at the Albertus University in Königsberg. His problem had nothing to do with mazes: given a mesh of resistors with more loops in it than a person can hold in their head, which of the equations you can write about it are independent of the others? The answer he published that year in *Annalen der Physik und Chemie* — build a matrix from the network, strike out one row and its matching column, take the determinant — is still how it is done.\n\nThe determinant turns out to be a count. It counts the ways of choosing wires so that every junction is reached and no loop is closed.\n\n:::note Kirchhoff was not counting anything\nHe wanted the rank of a system of equations, and the count is what his determinant happens to equal. He never wrote it down as a result. Kirby, Mallion, Pollak and Skrzyński went back through the 1847 paper to separate what he did from what has since been credited to him (*Croatica Chemica Acta* 89(4), 2016).\n:::\n\nReach every cell, close no loop. That is a **perfect maze** — exactly one route between any two points — and Kirchhoff's determinant counts those too.\n\n## The population\n\nGrid of cells, then the number of mazes it can hold: 2×2 gives 4. 3×3 gives 192. 4×4 gives 100,352. 5×5 gives 557,568,000. 6×6 gives 32,565,539,635,200. The sequence is A007341 in the OEIS, and its own entry names them as mazes.\n\nThe grid below is 28 by 17 out of the box. Its count is a 224-digit number.\n\n## Three builders\n\n**Recursive Backtracker** walks, carving into any unvisited neighbour it likes; boxed in, it reverses along its own path to the last cell with an exit left. **Randomized Prim** keeps a frontier of cells touching the maze, picks one at random and joins it. **Randomized Kruskal** shuffles every wall in the grid, then knocks each down in turn unless the two sides are already connected.\n\nAll three finish with a perfect maze on every seed. Switching builder rebuilds the current seed, so the size and the random stream stay fixed and only the rule changes — and what comes out does not look like the same problem.\n\n{{embed}}\n\n## What two thousand seeds say\n\nThe route between two cells in a perfect maze is unique, so BFS, A\\* and DFS all return the same one; they differ only in how much of the grid they trample finding it. Corner to corner on the 28×17 grid, averaged over two thousand seeds:\n\n- **Recursive Backtracker** — 184 cells of path, about 50 dead ends.\n- **Randomized Kruskal** — 68 cells, 144 dead ends.\n- **Randomized Prim** — 48 cells, 166 dead ends.\n\nThe shortest a corner-to-corner route can be on that grid is 44 cells. Prim's average sits three and a half cells above the floor; the backtracker's is more than four times it.\n\n:::warn Randomized Prim is not Prim's algorithm\nPrim's 1957 method (*Bell System Technical Journal* 36(6)) and Kruskal's 1956 one (*Proceedings of the AMS* 7(1)) both return the minimum spanning tree, and with distinct edge weights that tree is unique — so given the same random weights the two cannot disagree. The maze variant picks a random *cell* off the frontier rather than a random-weight edge, and the 48-against-68 gap above is the size of that substitution.\n:::\n\n## Fourteen\n\nDifferent textures mean different distributions over the same fixed population. A million seeds of each builder on a 3×3 grid, checking which of the 192 mazes ever come out:\n\nKruskal reaches all 192, its commonest maze about twice as often as its rarest. Prim reaches all 192 as well, far less evenly — its favourite comes up 2.27% of the time against a uniform 0.52%, and its rarest 0.0035% — six hundred times thinner than its favourite.\n\nThe Recursive Backtracker reaches 14.\n\n==Fourteen mazes. Not fourteen percent — fourteen out of 192, and the missing 178 are not rare, they are unreachable.==\n\nThe cause is a property of depth-first search that Robert Tarjan wrote down in 1972, in the paper that also gave us linear-time strongly connected components (*SIAM Journal on Computing* 1(2)). Run DFS on a connected undirected graph and it becomes what he named a palm tree: every edge the search declined to take joins a vertex to one of its own ancestors, never to a cousin. A backtracker's carve tree is a DFS tree, so every wall it leaves standing has to divide an ancestor from a descendant. Most mazes have a wall between cousins somewhere. Those mazes are legal and no seed will produce them.\n\nEnumerate the 3×3 spanning trees that satisfy Tarjan's condition from the top-left cell and there are 14 of them. The exhaustive count and a million runs of the generator above agree exactly.\n\n## Sampling evenly, and what it looks like\n\nAndrei Broder (FOCS 1989) and David Aldous (*SIAM Journal on Discrete Mathematics*, 1990) found the fix independently: walk the grid at random, keep the edge by which each cell is first entered, and the tree you end with is drawn uniformly. It is correct and it is slow, because the walk has to reach everything. David Bruce Wilson's cycle-popping algorithm (STOC 1996) gets the same guarantee faster, by erasing the loops out of random walks started from cells not yet in the tree.\n\nWilson's on the same 28×17 grid: 71 cells corner to corner, 138 dead ends. A fair sample of all the mazes measures like Kruskal's.\n\n>> The one maze-maker known to be unbiased produces the mazes that look least like mazes.\n\nA long winding corridor with few junctions is what a person draws when asked to draw a maze, and it is the signature of the single builder here that can reach 14 of 192. Jamis Buck's *Mazes for Programmers* (Pragmatic Bookshelf, 2015) tabulates dead ends and path lengths across a dozen algorithms and gives the random-walk pair a chapter of their own, titled \"Avoiding Bias with Random Walks\". The bias it names is what makes the backtracker's mazes look hand-drawn."
