    "slug": "conway-game-of-life",
    "title": "When Conway died, the obituaries led with the dots",
    "summary": "He found three sporadic simple groups, built a number line containing every infinity, and co-proved the free will theorem. When he died in 2020, the obituaries led with the dots.",
    "date": "2026-08-18",
    "embed": "game-of-life",
    "embedCaption": "Untick \"Wrap edges\" first — leave it on and the gliders come round the edge and back into the gun that made them. Then press Glider gun; it clears the board and starts itself. Now watch the \"Generation · cells\" readout under the grid: five more cells every thirty generations, one fresh glider each time. That counter is what cost Conway $50.",
    "published": true,
    "seoTitle": "Conway's Game of Life: the four rules and the $50 bet",
    "metaDescription": "Conway found the Game of Life on a Go board at Cambridge, bet $50 that no pattern could grow forever, and lost to Bill Gosper within weeks. Run it here.",
    "keywords": "conway game of life, john conway, gosper glider gun, martin gardner scientific american 1970, game of life rules, b3/s23, game of life turing complete, otca metapixel, surreal numbers, r-pentomino, lifeline newsletter",
    "content": "John Horton Conway died on 11 April 2020 in New Brunswick, New Jersey, of complications from COVID-19. He was 82 and had been at Princeton since 1987. The obituaries went up within a day — the New York Times, the Guardian, Princeton's own notice — and the Game of Life was in the first paragraph of nearly all of them, and in several of the headlines.\n\nHe had spent fifty years hoping people would ask about something else.\n\nIn 1968 he found three previously unknown sporadic finite simple groups inside the symmetries of the Leech lattice; they carry his name, Co₁, Co₂ and Co₃. Around 1970 he constructed the surreal numbers, one line containing the reals, the infinities and the infinitesimals alike, built out of a single recursive rule about games. In 1979 he and Simon Norton published the monstrous moonshine conjecture, tying the largest sporadic group to modular functions; Richard Borcherds proved it and took a Fields Medal for the proof in 1998. In 2006 he and Simon Kochen proved the free will theorem.\n\nStrangers wanted to talk about dots.\n\n>> He had built a number line with every infinity on it. His mail was about a five-cell shape that walks diagonally.\n\n:::warn How much he minded is contested\nConway told Brady Haran's Numberphile in 2014 that he had hated Life for years, then spent the rest of the interview explaining why he had stopped — the pattern space turned out to reach the questions about self-reproducing machines that von Neumann had left open. Siobhan Roberts records the ambivalence pointing both ways in her 2015 biography, *Genius at Play*. The version where he resented it to the last day is tidier than anything he said on the record.\n:::\n\n## Turing completeness, 1982 to 2010\n\nGliders travel. A gun emits a stream of them. Two streams can be aimed so that a glider in one annihilates a glider in the other, and that arrangement — one glider's survival conditioned on another's absence — is a logic gate. Berlekamp, Conway and Guy sketched a universal computer out of such parts in *Winning Ways for Your Mathematical Plays* in 1982. Paul Rendell had an explicit Turing machine built from Life patterns running by 2000, and a provably universal version with an unbounded tape in 2010. In 2006 Brice Due finished the OTCA metapixel, a Life pattern that behaves like a single Life cell, which is how you can watch Life running inside Life.\n\n==Nothing in the four rules mentions computation.== They were picked by a man pushing stones around a board, on the grounds that the results were hard to predict.\n\n## The $50 bet, November 1970\n\nConway offered $50 to the first person who could produce a pattern whose population grows without bound — one that keeps adding cells forever instead of dying out, freezing, or falling into a loop. He expected to keep the money.\n\n:::note The four rules\nA cell is alive or dead and looks at its eight neighbours. A live cell with two or three live neighbours survives; with fewer or with more it dies. A dead cell with exactly three live neighbours comes alive. Everything above and below follows from that. The shorthand is `B3/S23` — born on three, survives on two or three.\n:::\n\n{{embed}}\n\nBill Gosper, then at the MIT Artificial Intelligence Laboratory, found the counterexample in November 1970, weeks after the offer went out. His gun is thirty-six cells that return to their own starting configuration every thirty generations and, on each circuit, throw off a glider that sails away and never comes back. The population climbs without limit. Conway paid.\n\n## Gardner's column, October 1970\n\nThe offer had reached Gosper because Martin Gardner printed it. Gardner's Mathematical Games column in the October 1970 *Scientific American* was titled \"the fantastic combinations of John Conway's new solitaire game 'life'\", and it got out of hand immediately. He ran a follow-up in February 1971 to announce Gosper's gun. Robert Wainwright started a newsletter, *Lifeline*, in March 1971; readers posted in patterns they had found, and it ran eleven issues before folding in 1973.\n\nThe claim that Life ate a quarter of the world's computer time turns up in retelling after retelling and has never been traced to a source. Discount it. What survives inspection is smaller: an amateur readership formed around a rule set with no application and no way to win, and between them they found most of the patterns anyone still talks about.\n\n## The Go board, Cambridge, 1969\n\nBefore any of that there was a table in the mathematics department common room at Cambridge, and Conway and a group of students — Michael Guy among them — trying one rule set after another with a Go board and a bowl of stones.\n\nRules that let cells survive too easily flood the board into a static grey mush; rules that are too strict wipe everything out in a handful of generations. Conway wanted the band between them: no obvious way to make a pattern grow without limit, some patterns that appear to grow without limit anyway, and small starting shapes whose fates you could not talk your way to and had to run.\n\nTesting meant counting the neighbours of every stone, laying out the next generation, then clearing the old one. The R-pentomino is five cells and does not stop moving for 1,103 generations, throwing off six gliders before it settles at 116. Hands cannot follow that. The department's PDP-7 could.\n\nThe Go board was on the table because people played Go in that room, and Conway had been watching them. A Go endgame breaks into small independent regions that can be valued separately and added up, and that observation — a game as a sum of games, each with a number attached — is where the surreal numbers came from, in the same building, in the same two years.\n\nBoth came off the same board: black and white stones on a nineteen-by-nineteen grid, neighbours counted by hand. Fifty years later the obituaries led with one of them."
