    "slug": "turing-last-paper",
    "title": "Turing worked out how animals get spots in 1952. The experiment took another 38 years.",
    "summary": "His last published paper claimed a sheet of identical cells can break itself into spots, using nothing but two chemicals and the speeds at which they spread. He had no molecules and no evidence, and the reactor that showed he was right was not built for another thirty-eight years.",
    "date": "2026-08-18",
    "embed": "turing-bloom",
    "embedCaption": "Press Spots, then nudge the Kill slider left one notch at a time. The dots stretch into worms and then collapse into a maze — and the switch lands between two neighbouring slider positions, not gradually across the range.",
    "published": true,
    "seoTitle": "Turing Patterns: A 1952 Theory Nobody Could Test Until 1990",
    "metaDescription": "In 1952 Turing showed how two diffusing chemicals break uniform tissue into spots and stripes. The first proof came 38 years later, in a Bordeaux gel reactor.",
    "keywords": "reaction diffusion, turing pattern, morphogenesis, alan turing 1952, the chemical basis of morphogenesis, gray scott model, how animals get their spots, cima reaction 1990, castets de kepper, gierer meinhardt, kondo asai angelfish, zebrafish stripes, sheth hox digits, turing pattern wavelength",
    "content": "In 1951 Alan Turing was at the University of Manchester, using a computer to work out where an animal's spots come from. The machine was the Ferranti Mark I, delivered that February — the first computer sold commercially anywhere, and Turing had written its programmers' handbook. He wrote programs that stepped two imaginary chemicals around a ring of cells, letting them spread and react, printing the concentrations, watching for the moment a row of identical numbers stopped being identical.\n\nNobody else in biology was working this way. There were a dozen or so working stored-program computers on the planet, and Turing had written the software manual for one of them.\n\n## 1952\n\n*The Chemical Basis of Morphogenesis* appeared in *Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London B* in August 1952. It was the last paper Turing published in his lifetime. He had been arrested that February, convicted of gross indecency on 31 March, and sentenced to a year of oestrogen injections in place of prison.\n\nThe argument is short. Suppose tissue holds two chemicals — Turing called them morphogens — that diffuse and react. The first is an activator: it makes more of itself, and it makes the second as well. The second is an inhibitor: it shuts the first one down. ==Everything turns on the inhibitor diffusing faster than the activator.==\n\nThat should produce nothing. Diffusion is the universe's smoothing operator; drop ink in water and wait. Two diffusing chemicals ought to finish more uniform than they started. At the right rates they do the reverse. A chance flicker of extra activator amplifies itself and floods its neighbourhood with inhibitor, which travels further than the activator can and forbids any rival patch nearby — and thins with distance, so at some remove a second patch is free to grow. The forbidding sets a spacing. Spots, at a wavelength fixed by two diffusion constants and the reaction between them, with no map anywhere and no cell that knows the plan.\n\n{{embed}}\n\nWhich pattern comes out — dots, worms, a labyrinth, a field that never settles — depends on where you sit in the parameter space, and the borders between the regions are sharp. The equations running above are Gray and Scott's, written up in 1983 for stirred chemical reactors and turned into a pattern zoo by John Pearson in *Science* in 1993. The instability driving them is Turing's.\n\nHe had no candidate chemicals for any of it, and said so in the paper:\n\n> This model will be a simplification and an idealization, and consequently a falsification.\n>\n> — Alan Turing, *The Chemical Basis of Morphogenesis*, 1952\n\n## The gap\n\nWatson and Crick published the structure of DNA in *Nature* the following April, and development became a genetics problem. The question of what tells a cell where it is acquired an answer that sounded finished. Against genes, a theory of unnamed chemicals proposed by a mathematician with no standing in biology read as arithmetic about an imaginary animal.\n\nThe mathematics kept moving — Alfred Gierer and Hans Meinhardt rebuilt the activator–inhibitor scheme in Tübingen in 1972 and used it to model how a hydra regrows its head. What never arrived was an experiment. No pair of real chemicals in a real vessel had been shown to hold a stationary pattern, so nothing separated Turing's theory from a wrong one, and it was filed with the wrong ones.\n\nTuring was still working on morphogenesis when he died on 7 June 1954, at 41. He left unfinished manuscripts extending the theory to phyllotaxis — leaf arrangement, and the Fibonacci counts in a sunflower head. They were published in 1992, in the morphogenesis volume of his *Collected Works*.\n\n## 1990\n\nVincent Castets, Étiennette Dulos, Jacques Boissonade and Patrick De Kepper, at the Centre de Recherche Paul Pascal in Bordeaux, ran a chlorite–iodide–malonic acid reaction inside a strip of gel fed continuously from both edges. Rows of stationary spots appeared and stayed. They did not travel, did not fade, and their spacing was set by the chemistry rather than by the shape of the container. *Physical Review Letters* carried it in 1990.\n\nWhat had defeated everyone before was the rate difference. Small molecules in free solution diffuse at similar speeds, so no ordinary pair behaves like Turing's activator and inhibitor. The Bordeaux reactor contained starch, put there as a colour indicator; the starch bound the iodide species and dragged it back, supplying by accident the asymmetry the theory demanded. István Lengyel and Irving Epstein worked out that this was the mechanism the following year.\n\n## 1995\n\nShigeru Kondo and Rihito Asai photographed marine angelfish, *Pomacanthus*, as they grew, and published the sequence in *Nature* in August 1995.\n\nAn emperor angelfish is striped. If those stripes were a picture painted on the animal, they would get wider as the animal did. ==They stay the same width, and new stripes appear in the gaps between the old ones.== Kondo and Asai reproduced the insertions in a reaction–diffusion model. A blueprint has to name where each stripe goes; this fish is recomputing the answer as it grows.\n\n:::warn What the fit does not prove\nA pattern that a reaction–diffusion model reproduces has not been shown to be one. For most animal markings the two morphogens are still unidentified, and where the mechanism has been pinned down it has not always been chemistry: in zebrafish the activator and inhibitor turn out to be pigment cells signalling by direct contact rather than molecules diffusing through tissue (Inaba, Yamanaka and Kondo, *Science*, 2012). Turing's mathematics held. His molecules were a guess, and he labelled them one.\n:::\n\n## 2012\n\nRushikesh Sheth, James Sharpe, María Ros and colleagues deleted Hox genes from mouse limbs, cluster by cluster, and counted fingers. Removing instructions should remove digits. The mice grew more of them, each one thinner and packed tighter against its neighbours — which is what a Turing pattern does when its wavelength shortens. *Science* published it in December 2012.\n\nThe way to give a mouse extra fingers is to shorten a wavelength."
