    "embed": "murmuration",
    "embedCaption": "Take cohesion to zero and watch the flock boil off into a gas of birds politely avoiding one another; take separation to zero and watch it collapse to a single dot. Then find the band between them where it actually flies as a flock, and see how narrow that band is.",
    "published": true,
    "seoTitle": "Boids: Three Rules for a Flock — What Starlings Do Instead",
    "metaDescription": "Craig Reynolds' 1987 boids model flies a flock on three local rules. Real starlings over Rome track a fixed six or seven neighbours. Run it, and break it.",
    "keywords": "boids algorithm, craig reynolds, flocking simulation, murmuration, separation alignment cohesion, starling flock, topological distance, starflag, emergent behaviour, collective behaviour",
    "content": "You are a starling over Rome, half an hour before dusk in January, inside a roost flight coming in above the railway station.\n\nYour eyes sit on the sides of your head. That buys a field of view close to a full circle and costs you a wedge directly behind. Inside the circle: birds. Ahead of you a wall of them, everything past the first few rows hidden behind the rows in front. Above, below, both flanks, the same. Your whole working knowledge of this evening is a handful of neighbours and the gaps between them.\n\nYou cannot see the flock. From inside it the flock is not an object that appears in your visual field, and it never has been, and the same holds for every bird near you. ==Nothing in the sky tonight holds a picture of the flock, the flock included.==\n\n## Anaheim, 1987\n\nCraig Reynolds spent the 1980s at the Symbolics Graphics Division in Los Angeles, and in July 1987 he carried a paper to SIGGRAPH in Anaheim: *Flocks, Herds, and Schools: A Distributed Behavioral Model*. Behind it was a budget problem. A flock is hundreds of birds, and scripting hundreds of flight paths by hand is not work anyone finishes.\n\nSo Reynolds stopped writing the flock. He wrote one bird and ran a few hundred copies of it. The simulated birds he called **boids**, short for \"bird-oid object\" and pronounced the way a stereotyped New Yorker says \"bird\". They flew in front of an audience at the SIGGRAPH '87 Electronic Theater, in a short called *Stanley and Stella in: Breaking the Ice*. Five years later, flocking software of that lineage moved the bat swarms and penguin ranks of *Batman Returns*, on which Reynolds holds a screen credit. In 1998 the Academy gave him a Scientific and Engineering Award for his contributions to three-dimensional computer animation.\n\nWhat he handed each bird is what you have. Three instructions, one at a time.\n\n## Three instructions\n\n**One: when a neighbour comes too close, steer away from it.** Run that alone across a few hundred birds and you get a cloud that declines to collide and goes nowhere in particular. Order of a sort. No shape.\n\n**Two: steer toward the average heading of the neighbours you can see.** You know nothing about where the birds beyond them are pointing. But your neighbours are matching their neighbours, who are matching theirs, and a heading nobody selected begins crossing birds that never perceive each other.\n\n**Three: steer toward their average position.** This one stops the edges leaking. Agreement on direction will not hold a body together on its own — without a pull inward, birds at the margin drift off still perfectly aligned with a flock they have left.\n\nThat is the model: three steering forces, weighted and summed, computed from the birds in range. Nothing in those three sentences mentions turning as one body, or splitting around a chimney and closing behind it, or the density waves that travel through a murmuration like weather. You cannot get from the sentences to that by staring harder at the sentences.\n\n{{embed}}\n\nThree forces is the whole of it. Everything else in the picture follows from who counts as a neighbour.\n\n:::note What counts as \"in range\"\nReynolds did not hand a boid the whole world. Each one perceives neighbours inside a limited distance *and* a limited angle — a sphere with a wedge cut out of the back, matching the blind spot that eyes on the sides of a head leave you. Change that neighbourhood and you change the flock more than any weight does.\n:::\n\n## Six or seven neighbours\n\nNow leave the bird. You are on the roof of the Palazzo Massimo, a museum beside Roma Termini, on an evening between December 2005 and February 2006, beside pairs of stills cameras mounted 25 metres apart and fired in sync at ten frames a second.\n\nMichele Ballerini, Andrea Cavagna, Irene Giardina and the rest of the STARFLAG group photographed the roost flights in stereo, matched individual birds between the two images, and reconstructed three-dimensional positions for every member of flocks a few thousand strong. PNAS published the result on 29 January 2008. It let them put to real birds the question the simulation had assumed away: which neighbours is a starling responding to?\n\nReynolds had assumed distance. Everything inside a radius — that is what \"nearby\" means, and it is the obvious thing to implement.\n\nThe starlings count instead. Each bird interacts on average with its six or seven nearest neighbours, whatever the distance to them happens to be.\n\n>> Six is six whether the flock is a cloud or a wall.\n\nDensity inside a murmuration is not uniform and does not hold still. A radius rule breaks in both directions: pack the birds tighter and each one is suddenly tracking hundreds, thin them out and it tracks nobody while the flock shears apart. A rule that counts neighbours never notices either happening — the durable version, arrived at some millions of years before Anaheim, by birds with no way to check whether it works.\n\n:::warn What the falcon argument rests on\nPeregrines hunt these roosts, and a strike makes the flock contract, expand and split — the density swings a metric rule cannot absorb. The 2008 paper argues from that, and supports it with numerical simulation: topological flocks hold together under a modelled predator where metric ones fragment. That is an argument from a model. Nobody has stereo footage of a real strike.\n:::\n\nThe murmuration over Termini has been photographed from a rooftop, reconstructed in three dimensions and printed in a journal. The one vantage point it has never been visible from is inside it."
