    "slug": "perlin-noise",
    "title": "Nothing real is as clean as the surfaces in Tron",
    "summary": "Ken Perlin spent 1982 rendering light cycles at a graphics shop in Elmsford, New York, and every surface came out mathematically perfect. The function he wrote to spoil them sits underneath nearly every synthetic mountain and plume of smoke made since.",
    "date": "2026-08-18",
    "embed": "flow-field",
    "embedCaption": "Drag Detail to the bottom and watch a few thousand particles collapse into two or three vast sweeps; push it to the top and the same field combs them into hundreds of fine ones. Nothing here is choreographed — each particle only ever reads the angle at the pixel under it — and typing the seed back in redraws the piece exactly.",
    "published": true,
    "seoTitle": "Perlin Noise: Why random() Cannot Make a Mountain",
    "metaDescription": "Ken Perlin rendered light cycles for Tron in 1982 and hated how unblemished every surface looked. His fix: pseudo-random gradients blended across a lattice.",
    "keywords": "perlin noise, ken perlin, gradient noise, procedural generation, flow field, simplex noise, value noise, procedural terrain, fractional brownian motion, improved perlin noise, tron 1982, siggraph 1985",
    "content": "In 1982 Ken Perlin was in Elmsford, New York, rendering frames for *Tron*. The shop was Mathematical Applications Group, Inc. — MAGI — and its renderer, SynthaVision, built the world out of solid primitives: spheres, cylinders, cones and boxes, added to and subtracted from one another. That is why the light cycles look the way they do, and why every surface it produced was exact to the arithmetic.\n\nPerlin looked at the frames and found them ugly, and could say why. Nothing in shot had a blemish. No pitting in the metal, no dust settled into a corner, no scuff along the edge a thousand hands would have touched. The cel animation cut in around the rendered footage carried wear in every square inch. The rendered footage carried none, and a surface nothing has ever touched reads as a picture of a surface.\n\nThe remedy on offer was the texture map: an image painted by hand and wrapped onto each object. Paint a mountain that way and you have one mountain, at one resolution, with a seam down the side where the image meets itself.\n\n## What random() gives you\n\nThe obvious repair is noise — perturb every point on the surface by an independent random amount. That turns the surface to television static.\n\nTake a hillside instead. Two points a metre apart sit at almost the same height. Two points a hundred metres apart tell you nothing about each other. The surface is irregular at every scale and continuous at every scale, and it is the second property that static throws away. Wood grain has both. So does a coastline, and the rust on a bolt.\n\n>> Randomness has no notion of \"near\". Mountains do.\n\n`random()` returns a number bearing no relation to the one before it — the definition of the function, and the property that makes it useless here.\n\n## Randomness on a lattice\n\nThe function Perlin wrote in 1983 moves the randomness off the query and onto a lattice. Every integer point in space gets a fixed pseudo-random gradient — a direction, derived by hashing the coordinates. To evaluate the function anywhere, take the vector from each surrounding lattice corner to your point, dot it with that corner's gradient, and blend the results along an ease curve.\n\n==Two nearby query points fall inside the same lattice cell, so they blend the same handful of corner gradients, so their values come out close.== No smoothing pass runs afterwards. The values arrive correlated because of how they are built.\n\nNothing is stored, either. The gradients are recomputed from the coordinates on every call, so the function is defined at every point in space, at any zoom, and returns the same value for the same input on any machine and in any frame of a sequence.\n\n## Octaves\n\nOne layer gives a slow undulation about the size of the lattice. The mountain comes from adding the function to itself: sample it again at twice the frequency and half the amplitude, then again, then again. Four or five terms gets you ridgelines, then boulders, then grit.\n\n:::note The name for it\nStacking scaled copies this way is **fractional Brownian motion**, fBm in the shader literature. Its two knobs have names: *lacunarity* is the factor the frequency multiplies by each octave, usually 2; *persistence* is the factor the amplitude shrinks by, usually 0.5.\n:::\n\nPerlin published the whole thing as *An Image Synthesizer* at SIGGRAPH 1985. The showpiece was a marble vase — noise fed through a sine wave, so the bands wander instead of running straight — and the same paper handed out **turbulence**, the sum of the absolute values of the octaves, which is where the creased, billowing look of smoke and flame comes from.\n\n## Angles instead of heights\n\nSample the noise across a grid, read each value as an angle, and every point on the plane now carries an arrow. Release a few thousand particles into that field and they comb themselves into long curving streams.\n\n{{embed}}\n\nThe streams curve because neighbouring samples give neighbouring angles: a particle crossing the field is handed a heading that turns a little at a time. It has no memory and no destination — the whole shape of the stream is sitting in the field it walks over. Hand those same particles a fresh random angle at every pixel and they vibrate on the spot.\n\n:::warn Most \"Perlin noise\" is not\nTwo other functions ship under Perlin's name. **Value noise** puts random *values* at the lattice points and interpolates between them; its peaks and troughs land on the lattice, which surfaces as a faint square grid in anything built from it. **Simplex noise** is Perlin's own later replacement, built on a triangular grid so the cost stops doubling with every dimension added. Perlin noise interpolates gradients, and its lattice points are exactly where the function is zero.\n:::\n\n## Terrain, cloud, fire\n\nRead the value as a height and you have terrain: Minecraft's world generator is layered noise, which is why its overhangs and caves are found rather than authored. The same numbers read as density through a volume are cloud, and used to displace a flow they are fire. Sample with time as an extra axis and you get the wobble that keeps a procedural animation from looking mechanical.\n\nIn 1997 the Academy gave Perlin a Technical Achievement Award for it — a certificate, handed over at the science and technical dinner. *Tron* had collected two nominations of its own back in 1983, for costume design and for sound, and none for anything to do with how it looked.\n\n## Improving Noise, 2002\n\nSeventeen years after the original paper, by then a decade into teaching at NYU, Perlin published a two-page correction to his own function. The blend he used in 1985, 3t² − 2t³, is smooth, but its second derivative does not vanish at the lattice points. In a heightfield you cannot see that. In a bump map you see it at once, because bump mapping works on the derivative: a faint rectilinear grid, the ghost of the lattice, printed across the surface.\n\nHe replaced the curve with 6t⁵ − 15t⁴ + 10t³, whose first and second derivatives are both zero at either end, and threw out the random gradient directions in favour of twelve fixed ones pointing at the midpoints of a cube's edges. Random directions clump, and a clump of gradients all pointing one way is a streak."
